-- title:
--   Coefficient extension commutes with q↦ qⁿ
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure, let $n$ be a nonzero natural number and let $x$ be a formal Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, that is an element of `LaurentSeries ℚ` $=$ `HahnSeries ℤ ℚ`. Two ring homomorphisms are involved. First, [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) is [`ModularCurve.coeffMap`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) applied to the structure map $\mathbb{Q}\to L$: it is the coefficientwise ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Q}((q))\to L((q))$ sending a Hahn series to its image under the map induced on coefficients, $\sum_k a_k q^k\mapsto\sum_k \iota(a_k)q^k$. Second, for a commutative ring $R$, [`ModularCurve.qExpand R n`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) is the ring homomorphism on `LaurentSeries R` obtained by embedding the index domain along the order-preserving injective additive map $k\mapsto nk$ on $\mathbb{Z}$, i.e. the substitution $q\mapsto q^n$. The assertion is the equality of the two composites at $x$: applying $q\mapsto q^n$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and then extending coefficients to $L$ gives the same element of $L((q))$ as extending coefficients first and then applying $q\mapsto q^n$ over $L$.
--
--   A compatibility between the two basic operations on $q$-expansions used throughout the treatment of modular curves: extension of the field of coefficients and the degeneracy substitution $q\mapsto q^n$. It is invoked whenever $q$-expansion identities over $\mathbb{Q}$ are transported to a larger coefficient field, for instance in the analysis of level structures and of the fields generated by $q$-expansions of modular functions.
